{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.BrunnianHypothesisNotSatisfied","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.BrunnianHypothesisNotSatisfied","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mass.CrossingModelPairLocality","lean:module:QOU.Mass.BrunnianBindingChannel"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BrunnianHypothesisNotSatisfied.noIsolatedPairEnergy_forces_both","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BrunnianHypothesisNotSatisfied.noIsolatedPairEnergy_forces_both","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BrunnianHypothesisNotSatisfied.lean#L107-L114","doc":"**The two refutations agree**, which is the point worth recording: the\nstructural route (a crossing pair has positive count-channel energy) and the\nempirical route (AME measures `b₂ ≠ 0`) independently deny the same hypothesis.\nStated as the contrapositive: a `Brunnian` model has BOTH a vanishing pair\nbinding and no crossing-pair count-channel energy. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BrunnianHypothesisNotSatisfied.not_noIsolatedPairEnergy_of_countChannel","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BrunnianHypothesisNotSatisfied.not_noIsolatedPairEnergy_of_countChannel","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BrunnianHypothesisNotSatisfied.lean#L89-L105","doc":"**The structure-side refutation: a crossing pair at `q > 1` refutes `Brunnian`.**\n\nIf a pair's isolated mutual energy is the count channel of a topologically\nBrunnian pair — which is exactly what `brunnian_energy_is_count_channel` says it\nis, the signed channel being empty — then for any actual crossing (`0 < m`) at a\nsubstrate modulus `q > 1` that energy is strictly positive, so `w₂ ≠ 0`.\n\nL6a4 is a crossing pair: its six crossings distribute two to each component pair,\nwith `n₊ = n₋ = 1`, so `m = 1 > 0`.  Hence `CrossingModel.NoIsolatedPairEnergy` fails for it\non structural grounds alone, with no appeal to data. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.BrunnianHypothesisNotSatisfied.not_noIsolatedPairEnergy_of_pair_binding_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.BrunnianHypothesisNotSatisfied.not_noIsolatedPairEnergy_of_pair_binding_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/BrunnianHypothesisNotSatisfied.lean#L76-L87","doc":"**The data-side refutation: a nonzero pair binding refutes `Brunnian`.**\n\nAME2020 measures `b₂ = 2.2245662` MeV\n(`computations/probes/pair-locality-shortfall.witness.json`), and the pair\ncumulant of the induced binding map is `−w₂`.  So the measured two-body channel\nrefutes `CrossingModel.NoIsolatedPairEnergy` on the physical carrier — the same move\n`not_pairLocal_of_b3_ne_zero` makes for the three-body channel, one hypothesis\ndown. ","signatureChunk":0}]}